+# Tool Input Validation
+
+| Field  | Value                |
+| ------ | -------------------- |
+| Issue  | re-cinq/HALEngine#37 |
+| Status | Draft                |
+
+Tool input is model output: it reaches `ToolRegistry.execute` shaped by nothing but the model's guess at the JSON Schema the providers advertise, and today the registry forwards it to the executor unchecked. This spec pins the registry as the one place that gate lives — a call the declared `inputSchema` rejects is answered there, before any executor runs, in a form the model can read and correct — so every tool in the engine receives input the schema already accepts, and no consumer has to re-decide the question per executor.
+
+## The contract
+
+- `ToolRegistry.execute` validates the call's `input` against the resolved definition's `inputSchema` before the executor runs; input the schema rejects is answered with a `ToolResponse` whose `result` describes the failure, and the executor never runs ([validated by: answers input the schema rejects with a rejection naming the required field, leaving the executor unrun](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.acceptance.test.ts#L27)).
+- Input the schema accepts reaches the executor exactly as the model sent it: a property the schema does not list passes through unmodified, and nothing is coerced, defaulted, added or removed ([validated by: hands valid input to the executor exactly as the model sent it, unlisted properties included](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.acceptance.test.ts#L39)).
+- A rejection message names the failing property's path and the constraint that failed, and carries no value from the rejected input ([validated by: rejects a wrongly typed field naming its path and expected type, without echoing the value](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.acceptance.test.ts#L51)).
+- A rejected call leaves its input's values out of the log: no line the engine emits for the call carries the payload's data ([validated by: answers a rejected call without copying its input into the result or any log line](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.acceptance.test.ts#L63)).
+- `register` compiles a static definition's schema once, at registration, so a schema no validator can compile throws there with a message naming the tool ([validated by: refuses to register a schema no validator can compile, naming the tool at registration](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.acceptance.test.ts#L87)).
+- A function-valued `ToolDefinitionSource` is resolved per call, and each call is validated against the schema it returns at that call ([validated by: validates a function-valued definition against the schema it returns at that call](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.acceptance.test.ts#L97)).
+- A static tool's schema is compiled once and the compiled validator reused: ten `execute` calls compile it no second time ([validated by: compiles a static tool schema once and reuses the compiled validator across ten executes](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.acceptance.test.ts#L118)).
+- A rejection is a tool result, not an exception: the orchestrator feeds it back to the provider as the failed call's `tool_result`, the turn completes on a corrected retry, and the tool-round bound is what stops a retry cycle from spinning ([validated by: feeds a schema-rejected tool call back to the provider and finishes the turn on the corrected retry](../../src/orchestration/chatOrchestrator.acceptance.test.ts#L19)).
+- An unregistered name still throws `Unknown tool: <name>`, unchanged by validation ([validated by: still throws Unknown tool for an unregistered name](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.acceptance.test.ts#L132)).
+
+## Rationale
+
+`example/weather-tool.ts` shows the careful version of leaving the check to executors: it declares `location` as a required string, then casts and defaults it, and still answers a malformed call with a confidently formatted forecast for `'Unknown'`. Every consumer pushes the same question onto its own executor, differently each time or not at all. The schema is already handed to both providers verbatim, so the validator has to read that same schema — not a second copy in another format that drifts from what the model was shown.
+
+A rejection has to be a returned `ToolResponse` rather than a throw because of where the throw lands: it propagates through the tool-call `Promise.all`, out of the processing loop's `try`, fires `onError`, and reaches the WebSocket error handler, which sends a generic "Failed to process message" and never sends the stream-end frame. A readable rejection turns a malformed call into a correction the model makes inside the existing `DEFAULT_MAX_TOOL_ROUNDS = 5` budget.
+
+## Out of scope
+
+- The `Unknown tool: <name>` throw changing shape. Returning an unknown name to the model instead is the opposite treatment of the same failure class and is its own issue; changing it affects direct callers.
+- Validating tool _output_; hallucination detection; an evaluation harness.
+- Per-tool authorization. A schema-valid call for someone else's data is still valid, and authorization is a separate `ToolContext` boundary concern that no issue in this epic owns — _Let a consumer decline a tool call through a `beforeToolCall` hook_ is a veto seam, not an authorization model.
+- Retry, timeout and rate limiting around tool execution; any actual tool implementation, which is written against this contract later.
+- Anything beyond `required`, declared types and `enum`: no `additionalProperties: false` injection, no type coercion, no defaults, and no opt-out flag.
